1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:19,400
This is the shortest module in the course and the most conceptually demanding. It covers the
language features that separate code that works from code that scales — and, critically, how
to stop your app freezing while it waits for something.

2
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:25,400
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:37,800
First, closures. A closure is a self-contained block of code you can pass around like a
value. If you wrote a computed property in the last module, you've already written one.

4
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:51,800
There's a distinction worth knowing: escaping versus non-escaping. A non-escaping closure
runs and finishes inside the function. An escaping one outlives the function call — it's
stored somewhere and called later. Network callbacks are escaping.

5
00:00:51,800 --> 00:01:04,200
Why care? Because if you do slow work synchronously, the interface freezes while it waits.
The user thinks your app has hung. Escaping, asynchronous work is what keeps the screen
alive.

6
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:11,400
Next, three operations you'll reach for constantly. And I want to be precise here, because
these get confused.

7
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:20,600
Map transforms. It runs a closure on every element and gives you back a collection of the
same length. It does not search.

8
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:26,600
Filter selects. It keeps the elements matching a condition, so you get a shorter collection.

9
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:31,000
Reduce collapses. It folds a whole collection down into one value.

10
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:43,800
And if you want to find something, that's a fourth operation — first-where. Worth separating
those in your head now, because mixing up map and search is a very common early confusion.

11
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:58,600
Here they are doing real work: this filters the movie list by the search text, then sorts
the result by rating. Two operations, chained, replacing what would otherwise be a couple of
loops and a temporary array.

12
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:08,600
Now memory. Swift uses Automatic Reference Counting — ARC. It counts how many things are
holding onto an object, and frees it when nothing is.

13
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:27,400
A strong reference keeps an object alive. A weak reference doesn't, and is automatically set
to nil when the object goes away. There's also 'unowned', which doesn't retain and is never
nilled — so if you access it after the object is gone, you crash. Prefer weak.

14
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:37,000
The problem this prevents is a retain cycle: two objects each holding the other strongly, so
neither can ever be freed. That's a leak.

15
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:52,200
One habit prevents most of them. When you write a closure that captures self, mark it 'weak
self' by default. It costs nothing when unnecessary and saves you when it isn't. Here it is
in the real app.

16
00:02:52,200 --> 00:03:00,600
Finally, concurrency. There's one rule and everything follows from it. Background threads do
the waiting. The main thread does the drawing.

17
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:16,200
Long work — fetching data, downloading images — goes on a background thread. But every
single UI update must come back to the main thread. Update the interface from a background
thread and you get undefined behaviour and crashes.

18
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:35,400
The older way of doing this is Grand Central Dispatch, and you'll see it in existing code.
The modern way is async and await. 'await' means: pause here, let everything else keep
running, and continue when the result arrives. It's much easier to read than nested

19
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:45,400
Now the part of this module that isn't code. Before you build anything real, you plan it —
and there's a four-step workflow worth adopting.

20
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:55,800
Requirements first: list every feature, unfiltered. Then a flowchart: turn those into paths
and decisions. Then a wireframe: sketch what each screen holds. Only then, code.

21
00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:15,800
This is the flowchart for the reference app. Notice what's interesting about it — it isn't
the happy path. It's the branches. Not signed in. No seat selected. Cancel and release the
seats. Those branches are where bugs live, and drawing them before you write code is the

22
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:30,200
The professional argument is simple: when your app gets tested, every path gets walked,
including the awkward ones. If you already mapped them, testing finds nothing you didn't
anticipate. A flowchart is defect prevention, not paperwork.

23
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,700
Let's recap the key points.

24
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:35,200
Here is your practice exercise.

25
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:42,400
In Module four the app starts talking to the internet — URLSession, JSON, and a real public
API.
